<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1500 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Wexford Carol</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old Irish Christmas carol from the town of Enniscorthy in County Wexford, about the birth of Jesus.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (Irish folk melody, transcribed by William Grattan Flood)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the people of Enniscorthy and County Wexford</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Enniscorthy, County Wexford, Ireland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>probably 15th or 16th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; probably of the 15th or 16th century, transcribed by William Grattan Flood (d. 1928))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford-carol">Wexford Carol</a>, also called the Enniscorthy Carol (in Irish Carúl Loch Garman or Carúl Inis Córthaidh), is a traditional Irish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> that comes from Enniscorthy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">County Wexford</a>. It is sometimes known by the opening of its first verse. Its subject is the nativity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a>.</p><p>Its origins are uncertain. It is occasionally claimed to date from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, but its musical and lyrical style suggest that it was more likely composed in the 15th or 16th century. Neither an author nor a composer is known.</p><p>The carol owes its renewed popularity to William Grattan Flood (1859–1928), organist and musical director at St. Aidan's Cathedral in Enniscorthy. He transcribed it from the performance of a local singer and had it published in The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Book of Carols, after which other carol books took it up as well.</p><p>Traditions have gathered around the song, for example that only men should sing it. Many well-known women singers have nevertheless recorded it, among them Julie Andrews in 1966, whose version has an additional verse, and Loreena McKennitt in 1987. Nanci Griffith recorded it for the Chieftains' 1989 album A Chieftains Celebration, and John Rutter recorded his arrangement in 2002 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> Singers and the baritone Stephen Varcoe. Yo-Yo Ma and Alison Krauss recorded it for Ma's 2008 album <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Joy &amp; Peace.</p><p>It is now sung by choirs and soloists well beyond Ireland. The English boys' choir Libera recorded an arrangement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> Cathedral for a 2013 album, the Mormon Tabernacle Choir sang an arrangement by its conductor Mack Wilberg on a 2016 album, and the Irish tenor Anthony Kearns performs it every year. In 2014 it gave its name to The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford-carol">Wexford Carols</a>, a collection of traditional Irish carols by the Irish early-music singer Caitríona O'Leary.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A carol that calls its hearers to remember the birth of Christ at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> and tells the nativity story in turn: the birth at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, the shepherds and the wise men. Its tone is devout and quietly joyful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Good people all, this Christmas time,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Consider well and bear in mind</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>What our good God for us has done</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In sending His beloved Son</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With Mary holy we should pray,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To God with love this Christmas Day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In Bethlehem upon that morn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There was a blessed Messiah born.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The night before that happy tide,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The noble virgin and her guide</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Were long time seeking up and down</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To find a lodging in the town.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But mark how all things came to pass</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From every door repelled, alas,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As was foretold, their refuge all</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Was but a humble ox's stall.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Near Bethlehem did shepherds keep</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their flocks of lambs and feeding sheep</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To whom God's angels did appear</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Which put the shepherds in great fear</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Prepare and go, the angels said</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To Bethlehem, be not afraid</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For there you'll find, this happy morn</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A princely Babe, sweet Jesus, born.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With thankful heart and joyful mind</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The shepherds went the babe to find</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And as God's angel had foretold</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They did our Saviour Christ behold</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Within a manger He was laid</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And by his side the virgin maid</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Attending on the Lord of Life</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who came on earth to end all strife.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There were three wise men from afar</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Directed by a glorious star</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And on they wandered night and day</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Until they came where Jesus lay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And when they came unto that place</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where our beloved Messiah lay</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They humbly cast them at His feet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With gifts of gold and incense sweet.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; the English words as transcribed by William Grattan Flood, as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wexford_Carol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
